# The bytes of the cross-repo contract artifacts are load-bearing, so they must not
# be rewritten on checkout.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the default on Windows here) converts LF to CRLF when files
# land in the working tree. For ordinary source that is harmless. For these two trees
# it breaks the distribution mechanism itself:
#
#   contracts/    consumers vendor a pinned copy of the schema plus a test asserting
#                 its sha256 matches the claimed version (IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md
#                 §5.1). Line-ending conversion changes the file's hash, so the SAME
#                 commit would present different sha256s on Windows and Linux and
#                 every vendored-copy check would fail across platforms — reported as
#                 contract drift, which is exactly the alarm it is supposed to raise
#                 only for real drift.
#
#   observations/ every line of an observation log hashes to its MANIFEST.csv row,
#                 and `supersedes` points at one of those hashes. A consumer in
#                 another language splitting on \n would carry a trailing \r into the
#                 hash and fail to verify a log that is in fact intact. The whole
#                 point of a language-neutral contract is that such a consumer works.
#
# Scoped to these two trees deliberately: the rest of the repo has no byte-level
# contract, and normalising it would be churn without a reason.
contracts/**    text eol=lf
observations/** text eol=lf

# Archived third-party payloads: `-text` disables newline conversion entirely, which
# is stronger than pinning eol=lf and is the right guarantee here. These files are a
# vendor's ORIGINAL bytes, held because the source restates them and the Internet
# Archive may not keep them; `data/vintages/*/MANIFEST.csv` records each one's sha256
# as its provenance. Normalising to LF would still be a rewrite — it would silently
# "fix" a payload the vendor happened to ship with CRLF, and the stored checksum would
# then attest to our edit rather than to what was published. Never reformat evidence.
data/vintages/** -text
